% ============================================================================
% lung-size-comparison — how the right lung compares in SIZE/SHAPE to the
% left lung, decoded from a clause already sitting unused inside
% `lung-lobes.adj`'s own already-quoted NCI SEER source sentence
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `lung-lobes.adj`
% (`lung_lobe_count(lung, count)`, right_lung → 3, left_lung → 2): that
% table's own header already quotes, verbatim, the NCI SEER sentence that
% fixes the right lung's lobe count AND, in the same breath, names three
% comparative descriptors for the right lung relative to the left -- a fact
% the lung/count schema had no room for:
%
%     "The right lung is shorter, broader, and has a greater volume than the
%      left lung. It is divided into three lobes and each lobe is supplied by
%      one of the secondary bronchi."
%
% New `lung_size_comparison(lung, comparison)` table decodes those three
% comparative descriptors as the right lung's own rows: shorter, broader,
% greater_volume. "Comparison" (a size/shape descriptor relative to the OTHER
% lung) is categorically distinct from the parent table's "count" (a plain
% lobe number) -- the right lung's count is 3, but its size comparisons are
% three separate qualitative facts the count column has no room for. Recall
% is a binding query:
%
%     ? lung_size_comparison(right_lung, $Comparison)  % shorter, broader, greater_volume
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `lung_size_comparison(lung, comparison)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the
% comparison WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on anything that is not the right lung (it never invents a comparison). The
% query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a comparison and recall the lung it
% describes:
%
%     ? lung_size_comparison($L, broader)  % right_lung
%
% A recall on `left_lung` -- a real, already-tabled lung in `lung-lobes.adj`,
% but whose own quoted span ("The left lung has two lobes.") states only its
% lobe count, never a comparative size descriptor -- correctly abstains
% rather than inventing a comparison for it. The source states these three
% comparisons only in the DIRECTION right-lung-relative-to-left-lung, never
% the reverse, so `left_lung` is deliberately left unrowed rather than
% inverted into a guessed "longer/narrower/lesser_volume" claim the source
% never makes.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME sentence already quoted
% inside `lung-lobes.adj`'s own header, from the SAME NCI SEER Training
% "Bronchi, Bronchial Tree, & Lungs" page that table already cites -- no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a different clause of that
% already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier the parent
% table already carries (NCI SEER Training is a primary U.S. government
% reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table lung_size_comparison {
    columns lung, comparison

    row (right_lung, shorter)
    row (right_lung, broader)
    row (right_lung, greater_volume)

    source "The right lung is shorter, broader, and has a greater volume than the left lung. It is divided into three lobes and each lobe is supplied by one of the secondary bronchi."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/bronchi.html"
    trust authoritative
}
